  "name": "Slow Roast Haunch of Venison with Shallots, cranberries, and chestnuts.",
  "text": "Slow Roast Haunch of Venison with Shallots, cranberries, and chestnuts.\n\nPreparation time: 15 minutes with 2 hours marinade\n\nCooking time: 4 hours\n\nYield: 6\n\nIngredients\n\n2 - 2 1/2 kg haunch of venison (bone in),\n\n12 banana shallots (peeled but whole),\n\n100g dried cranberries,\n\n150g vac-pack chestnuts (crumbled),\n\n300ml red wine,\n\n50g redcurrant jelly,\n\n750ml beef stock,\n\n2 tsp dried sage,\n\n200g bacon lardons,\n\nVegetable oil for cooking,\n\nVenison Rub\n\n2 tsp juniper,\n\n8 cloves,\n\n1 tbsp cracked black pepper,\n\n2 tbsp flakey sea salt,\n\n1 tbsp cumin seeds,\n\n2 tbsp thyme leaves,.\n\nPreparation\n\nLightly score the venison haunch with a sharp knife. In a pestle and mortar or spice grinder blend together the venison rub ingredients. Rub them into the haunch and leave for 2 hours.\n\nIn a large casserole pan, heat a layer of vegetable oil and cook the lardons until crispy brown.\n\nAdd the shallots and cook until they start to brown. Stir in the dried sage. Add the redcurrant jelly, cranberries, red wine, and stock. Bring to the boil and place the venison on top. Turn the heat off and cover with a lid or tin foil. Place in a pre-heated oven at 150C for 2 hours.\n\nAfter 2 hours check and add more stock if dry. Cook for another 2 hours.\n\nLeave to rest for 30 minutes.",
  "cookTime": "PT4H",
  "recipeYield": "6",
